course: dictionary
lesson: 7870
title: Rainforest: a word for a place
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A rainforest is a thick forest of tall trees in a place that rains a lot, almost every month. You can find the meaning by reading the sentences around the word. «The canopy is the thick roof of leaves and branches high in a rainforest.» The clue is "roof of leaves and branches high in", so the canopy is the top part of the forest, and a rainforest is a forest.
explain: Look for clues about where and how. «Tropical rainforests grow mostly close to the equator, where strong sun heats moist air, which rises, cools and drops heavy rain nearly every day.» Close to the equator, hot sun and heavy rain tell you a tropical rainforest is hot and wet all year.
explain: Some words tell you what lives there. «Tree kangaroos live in rainforests, climb with strong arms and can leap many metres down to the ground without getting hurt.» Tree kangaroos climb, so the rainforest must have tall trees for them to live in.
fact: A rainforest is a thick forest of tall trees where it rains a lot almost every month.
fact: Most rainforests are tropical: they are near the equator and stay hot all year.
fact: Some rainforests are temperate, which means cooler and wet, like those in New Zealand, Tasmania and the north-west of the USA and Canada.
fact: A rainforest has layers: the forest floor, the understorey, the canopy and the emergent layer at the very top.
fact: Rainforests help the whole planet by taking in carbon dioxide and giving out oxygen. The Amazon is the biggest rainforest.
exercise: «Tree kangaroos live in rainforests, climb with strong arms and can leap many metres down to the ground without getting hurt.» Where do tree kangaroos live? | In a desert with no trees | On the ice at the North Pole | Under the sea | *In a rainforest
exercise: «The canopy is the thick roof of leaves and branches high in a rainforest.» What is the canopy? | A wide, fast river that runs through the trees | *The thick roof of leaves and branches high in a rainforest | A kind of bird that lives in the mud | The dark forest floor where the leaves fall
exercise: «Tropical rainforests grow mostly close to the equator, where strong sun heats moist air.» Where do most tropical rainforests grow? | Near the North Pole, in the snow | Only on the tops of high mountains | Far from any rain, in the desert | *Close to the equator, where it is hot all year
test: «A tropical rainforest is a tower of homes in layers: a few giant emergent trees poke out at the very top, below them the canopy, a thick roof of leaves 30 to 45 metres up.» Which layer is the thick roof of leaves 30 to 45 metres up? | Forest floor | *Canopy | Understorey | Emergent layer
test: «Temperate rainforests, like those in New Zealand, are cooler and wet.» What does temperate mean for a rainforest? | Under the sea | Dry and dusty | *Cooler and wet | Hot all year
exam: Why are rainforests so important to the whole planet? | They make the sea salty | Their sand is used to make glass | They stop the wind from ever blowing | *They take in carbon dioxide and give out oxygen
